# Netlify / Cloudflare Pages header config. Vercel's equivalent lives in vercel.json;
# both set the same two headers so the Terminal panel's WebContainers fallback (a real,
# in-browser Node.js runtime, used only when no local backend is reachable — see
# README.md) can boot. Harmless if unused: these headers only matter to pages that opt
# into cross-origin isolation via JS, which the Terminal panel only does on its fallback path.
#
# GitHub Pages has no equivalent custom-headers mechanism, so this can't be replicated
# there — CodeForge deployed to GitHub Pages falls back to the plain simulated terminal
# instead, which is expected and handled gracefully.
/*
  Cross-Origin-Opener-Policy: same-origin
  Cross-Origin-Embedder-Policy: require-corp
